region   what                          address  explanation
-------- ------------------ -------------------  -----------
  .text    a_function()           0x648ce64c9336  machine code; r-x, executable but NOT writable
  .text    main()                 0x648ce64c941c  also code -- functions are not variables, they have no storage
  .rodata  "hello" literal        0x648ce64ca00c  string literals are read-only; modifying one is undefined behaviour
  .rodata  g_const                0x648ce64ca008  const global; the page itself is mapped read-only
  .data    g_initialized          0x648ce64cc010  nonzero initializer, so the value is stored inside the binary
  .data    s_initialized          0x648ce64cc014  static local: visible only in main(), but lives for the whole run
  .bss     g_zero                 0x648ce64cc080  zero-initialized, so the file stores only a size, not the bytes
  .bss     s_zero                 0x648ce64ce5a8  static local, also zeroed by the kernel before main() starts
  .bss     g_buffer[8192]         0x648ce64cc0a0  8 KB of zeros that add nothing to the on-disk binary size
  heap     malloc(64)             0x648d04b14010  small request: carved out of the brk heap, just above the globals
  heap     copy of a string       0x648d04b14060  the literal is in .rodata; this writable copy is on the heap
  mmap     malloc(16 MB)          0x71ef96d88010  over ~128 KB glibc skips the heap and mmaps a fresh region
  libc     printf()               0x71ef97df0300  not in your binary at all: shared library, mapped in at startup
  stack    local                  0x7ffc16d5791c  automatic variable, gone the moment main() returns
  stack    local_arr[64]          0x7ffc16d57960  arrays are laid out in the frame just like scalars
  stack    ptr_to_local           0x7ffc16d57920  the POINTER is on the stack, and so is what it points at
  stack    argc                   0x7ffc16d5790c  parameters are part of the frame too
  stack    argv[0] string         0x7ffc16d591a6  argv/environ strings sit at the very top of the stack
  stack    local @ depth 1        0x7ffc16d578e4  one frame further down -- note the address DROPS
  stack    local @ depth 2        0x7ffc16d578b4  one frame further down -- note the address DROPS
  stack    local @ depth 3        0x7ffc16d57884  one frame further down -- note the address DROPS

